course: dictionary
lesson: 3401
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so context tells you which one is right. «My bus ticket has expired» means the time it was allowed to be used has run out. «The milk has expired» means it is past its use-by date and no longer safe to drink.
explain: Expired can also be a cool, formal way of saying someone died. «The patient expired in the night» uses a technical word in place of the plain word died. Doctors and official reports use it, and it can sound cold, so listen to the tone and the topic.
explain: A fourth meaning is a science word for breathing out. You will rarely meet it in a story. If expired sits near words like lungs, mouse or air, that is the sense to think of. Always check the sentence and ask which meaning fits.
fact: Expired means that the time for something has come to an end.
fact: «My passport has expired» means it can no longer be used.
fact: «The yoghurt expired last week» means it is past its best-before date.
fact: Died is the plain word; expired is a softer, technical way of saying it, used in formal and medical language.
fact: In science, expired can mean breathed out.
exercise: «My library card has expired.» What does expired mean here? | It is full of new pages | It has just been printed | It has been lost in the rain | *Its time to be used has ended
exercise: «The patient expired in the night.» What plain word could replace expired? | recovered | *died | sneezed | slept
exercise: «Check the date: the cheese has expired.» Which sense fits? | *past its use-by date and not safe to eat | a very strong smell | it has been cut into pieces | a new brand of cheese
test: «The scientist measured the air each mouse expired.» What does expired mean in this science sentence? | ran out of food | *breathed out | fell asleep | shouted loudly
test: «The coupon expired on Friday, so the shop would not take it.» Why would the shop not take it? | It was printed in blue | It was too big | The shop was closed for the holiday | *Its time to be used had run out
exam: Ana says, «Expired always means died.» Is Ana right? | Yes, it always means dead | *No, it has several meanings, and context shows which one | No, it is only a word for food | Yes, but only in shops
